{"connection_id": "BC-cc658885170b590d1a6d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.20.32", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word and inheritance entrusted", "note": "As he departs, Paul commends the elders to God and the word of grace, which can build them up and give them an inheritance; this is a striking Christian scene of a departing teacher handing successors both a trust and an inheritance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eae1dc238683b9ebb154", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.20.32", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Paul's farewell entrusts elders to God", "note": "In his farewell Paul commends the elders to God and the word able to give inheritance; the departing speaker hands over care and names a future inheritance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f097d76b861134fa87eb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.16", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "teach and admonish one another", "note": "The community is to let Christ's word dwell richly and teach and admonish one another; a received word is actively passed among peers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-80d8aca8c371c41958e3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.1.13", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sealed after believing", "note": "Believers are sealed with the promised Spirit after hearing and believing the message; this adds a seal of authentication to the commentary's pairing of entrusted faith and a guarantor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cfd382307d0039d1b949", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.1.14", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guarantee of inheritance", "note": "The Spirit is called the pledge of the believers' inheritance until its redemption; this closely joins the commentary's guarantor standing behind a claim with heirs awaiting their due."}
{"connection_id": "BC-867e353f5f6f26e51242", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.1.14", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Spirit as pledge of inheritance", "note": "The Spirit is called a pledge of believers' inheritance until redemption; an assurance stands behind a promised future possession, connecting guarantor and bequest."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7e1361b7c0b829ba7af9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.4.15", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "truth spoken in love", "note": "The community is to speak truth in love so the body grows; truth becomes a reciprocal act rather than a private possession."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7224833948320ba21014", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.4.2", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "patience bears one another", "note": "Humility and patience are expressed by bearing with one another in love; the commentary's mutual recommendation of sabr is lived as responsibility for others."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fae1bff3a7c0873b7b63", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.4.25", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "truth between members", "note": "The letter commands each person to speak truth with their neighbor because they are members of one another; it closely matches the commentary's reciprocal counsel in truth and explains it through mutual belonging."}
{"connection_id": "BC-595130d06d031ca4d98a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Eph.6.4", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "parents bring children up in admonition", "note": "Fathers are told to raise children in the Lord's discipline and instruction; household advice is a sustained responsibility, as in the commentary's father-to-child counsel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e1e501d17393d85e32c1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.2", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bearing another's burden", "note": "Paul tells believers to bear one another's burdens; this gives a concrete communal form to the commentary's image of a guarantor standing behind another and carrying an obligation with them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-409e0d47ced9fc1f0f8b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.1.1", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God speaks through prophets across generations", "note": "God formerly spoke to the ancestors by prophets in many ways; it parallels the commentary's continuous chain of divine instruction to successive messengers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eb1afa6e0d945101cddb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.1.2", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God speaks in the Son in last days", "note": "The following verse presents speech through the Son in the last days; it marks a specifically Christian culmination beside the commentary's sequence from Noah to Jesus."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8d51df68057bc1eb862d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.24", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "stirring one another to good works", "note": "The readers are to consider how to provoke one another to love and good works; this directly joins reciprocal exhortation to the commentary's faithful people's good action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-af271067ed2f11dea835", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.24", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "provoke one another to good works", "note": "The assembly must consider how to stir one another to love and good works; the commentary's mutual charge has an enacted ethical goal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-85b861f3eb6d0115e49c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.25", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "communal encouragement", "note": "The readers are told to keep assembling and encourage one another; this makes mutual counsel an ongoing communal practice rather than a solitary last instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3b990c8b7ea7865bd723", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.25", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "keep assembling and encourage one another", "note": "Believers are told to encourage one another as the day approaches; counsel is repeated communally under awareness of limited time."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0dd362fa4395a62e7d6a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.21", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Jacob's final blessings interpreted as faith", "note": "Hebrews describes Jacob's blessing of Joseph's sons as an act of faith at death; it interprets the deathbed transfer as trust in a future he will not see."}
{"connection_id": "BC-30035e6a5abdad86c7d4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.22", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Joseph's final charge remembered", "note": "Hebrews recalls that Joseph, near death, gave instructions about his bones; it interprets the Genesis deathbed oath as an act of faith transmitted beyond the testator's life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2368fca3ff58a90e255a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.22", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Joseph's dying charge interpreted as faith", "note": "Hebrews interprets Joseph's instruction about his bones and Israel's exodus at death as an act of faith; it makes the generational obligation depend on confidence in a future event."}
{"connection_id": "BC-62a803dc48db9b55ef78", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.12", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith and patience inherit", "note": "The readers are urged to imitate those who through faith and patience inherit the promises; this uniquely combines the commentary's faith and ṣabr with its image of heirs receiving what is due."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aaa962c3a2fd1d4d24c7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.17", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "oath securing heirs' promise", "note": "God confirms the promise to its heirs with an oath; this directly combines the commentary's heirs and sworn testimony, showing an oath used to assure rather than test a claimant."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5c7cf8b32a15304f6a9b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.7.22", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guarantor of a covenant", "note": "Jesus is named guarantor of a better covenant; this supplies a distinct Christian use of surety for the commentary's figure who stands behind another's obligation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ca78f43fc90660e7261", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.7.22", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus guarantor of a better covenant", "note": "Jesus is called the guarantor of a better covenant; the commentary's kefil and binding inherited obligation meet in a person who stands behind a covenant."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ffc85ee82a4d11a5081f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.9.15", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "death, covenant, inheritance", "note": "The mediator's death is linked to the called receiving the promised eternal inheritance; Hebrews supplies a Christian interpretation in which death and inheritance belong together, as in the commentary's testament image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2f181a13d04a39dc5acd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.9.15", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "promised eternal inheritance", "note": "Christ mediates a new covenant so the called receive the promised eternal inheritance; the bequest image is used for a religious promise rather than divided property."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1d4565d7a2d83b20adc1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.9.16", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "death of the testator", "note": "Hebrews says a death is necessary for a testament to take effect; this makes explicit the legal logic behind the commentary's dying speaker leaving a binding word to survivors."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7785d23b5d35bb9cd7ff", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.9.16", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "death and testament", "note": "The argument says a testament requires the death of its maker; it places the commentary's deathbed transfer of binding words beside an explicit death and inheritance analogy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6c4e49d297d18b8c8491", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.9.17", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "testament effective at death", "note": "A testament is said to take effect only after the maker dies; this sharpens the commentary's metaphor of advice as a last bequest by explaining when such a bequest binds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a41b6dfb4674064b29f6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.9.17", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "testament takes force after death", "note": "The next verse says a testament becomes effective on death; it specifies the moment at which a speaker's word binds the living."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4cfad016e3891ce67517", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.10", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prophets as examples of patience", "note": "James points to prophets who spoke in the Lord's name as examples of suffering and patience; it unites the commentary's messenger chain with the sabr expected of those who inherit their charge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e8802c416b5b4871ceea", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.11", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Job's endurance remembered", "note": "The community is told it has heard of Job's endurance and seen the Lord's outcome; endurance is transmitted as a remembered example to later hearers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8507feac7d1be40f6547", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.12", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "plain speech instead of oath", "note": "The commentary's cited will procedure requires witnesses to swear, whereas James says not to swear and to let yes mean yes and no mean no; the contrast makes truthful speech without an oath an alternative account of reliable testimony."}
{"connection_id": "BC-732dcfe5235805406e79", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.12", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "truthful speech without oath", "note": "James forbids oaths and requires an unambiguous yes or no; the commentary's oath secures disputed bequest testimony by a legal act that James tells his audience to avoid."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e71b8b64c6b825c2f5f", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.13.34", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "farewell command to love one another", "note": "Jesus gives his disciples a command to love one another on the eve of his death; this is a concrete last instruction entrusted to a community, though its content differs from the commentary's truth and patience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bde3a26d9de9e4f70185", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.13.34", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "farewell command to love one another", "note": "At the farewell meal Jesus gives a new command to love one another; a departing teacher leaves a reciprocal obligation to his community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3a898eeffbf10b80c8d9", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.14.27", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "peace left with disciples", "note": "Jesus says he leaves and gives his peace to the disciples; the farewell gift formula gives a distinct Christian example of a legacy passed to survivors."}
{"connection_id": "BC-24ad3e61888b3f191368", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.17.20", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "future believers through disciples' word", "note": "Jesus prays at his farewell for people who will believe through his disciples' word; his instruction moves beyond those present to a future generation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5e7e9b7c42fc23268e9b", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.8.17", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two witnesses in Jesus' defense", "note": "Jesus cites the legal rule that the testimony of two people is true while defending his own witness; the estate-witness formula is reused in a different dispute over credibility."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba9c1df379cd59a9a4cc", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.8.18", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Father joins Son as witness", "note": "Jesus says he bears witness and the Father who sent him also bears witness; the preceding two-witness rule is given an unusual father-son application."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-fb03748f7e8c0fea4cac", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jude.1.3", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith handed over once for all", "note": "Jude urges a community to contend for the faith entrusted to the saints; a received deposit calls for continued action by those who inherit it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba147fbf8ee45f971f84", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.11.2", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus teaches prayer to followers", "note": "Jesus answers a disciple's request by giving words to pray; the Qur'anic command laid on Jesus becomes a practice he hands to his followers in this scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2b1d978fae9e72c0f5bc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.13", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "dispute over inheritance", "note": "A man asks Jesus to tell his brother to divide the inheritance with him; this directly stages the claim over heirs' shares that the commentary treats as rights."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8639f623f314ced34669", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.13", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "request to divide inheritance", "note": "A person asks Jesus to tell a brother to divide an inheritance; the commentary's regulated heirs' shares are here voiced as a contested family demand."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff6832a17df3daa02553", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.14", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "refusal to arbitrate inheritance", "note": "Jesus refuses the role of judge or divider in the inheritance dispute and warns against greed; this contrasts a legal allocation of heirs' rights with a warning that the claim itself can be spiritually misdirected."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a069d6585ba50f02f98b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.15", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "warning following inheritance dispute", "note": "Jesus warns the disputants against greed and says life does not consist in possessions; the estate scene adds a moral check on the commentary's concern for rightful shares."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e03ecc85e95789fed845", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.20", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "death interrupts accumulation", "note": "In the rich fool parable, God asks who will receive the goods the man prepared after his death; this counters confidence in a self-managed estate with the uncertainty of who inherits it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-045c8679b872939fc44f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.20", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "death interrupts plans for stored goods", "note": "God tells a man his life is required that night and asks who will own what he prepared; the commentary's warning that one may lose the chance to leave a bequest becomes an explicit question about successors."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4ed56715b13477ad7040", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.12", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "father divides property while alive", "note": "The younger son asks for his share and the father divides his property between them; it supplies a narrative test case for the commentary's distinction between a living father's gift and inherited portions."}
{"connection_id": "BC-04c4d86527241051088d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.31", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "father affirms elder son's share", "note": "The father tells the elder son that all he has is his; even after the younger son has spent his portion, the remaining heir's claim is explicitly preserved."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1c955110fda953551c02", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.22.20", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "farewell cup and new covenant", "note": "At the final meal Jesus calls the cup the new covenant in his blood; a departing speaker leaves a binding religious relation to his followers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3402151f7b51860eabe6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.22.32", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "strengthening others after trial", "note": "Jesus tells Peter that, once he turns back, he must strengthen his brothers; the instruction turns personal faith through a coming test into support passed to others, like the commentary's mutual counsel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-940fec518949b935757a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.18.16", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two or three witnesses establish a matter", "note": "Jesus cites the two or three witness rule when a private rebuke fails; the commentary's disputed testimony likewise requires a claim to be publicly established."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f4c0eedbee7f953725cd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.21.38", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "attempt to seize the heir's inheritance", "note": "The tenants identify the owner's son as heir and kill him in hopes of taking his inheritance; this counter-scene makes the commentary's concern with heirs' rights vivid through their violent denial."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bc85fc5dd44aa004cf84", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.24.44", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unexpected end allows no preparation", "note": "Jesus tells disciples to be ready because the Son of Man comes at an unexpected hour; this is an indirect analogue to the commentary's people overtaken before they can leave an instruction, although the verse concerns the Son of Man's coming."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4560233b22ea5386d1d0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.26.63", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus adjured under oath", "note": "The high priest adjures the detained Jesus by the living God to answer, and Jesus has been silent; a compelled religious oath appears in an interrogation, unlike the commentary's estate witnesses."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9e22a7190cb0f02e053a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.5.34", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Jesus forbids swearing oaths", "note": "Jesus instructs his hearers not to swear at all; the commentary's bequest witnesses are required to swear, so the texts put a different weight on compelled truth telling."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9e5e23fba197a06a80b8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.5.5", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "meek inherit the land", "note": "Jesus promises the land to the meek; this gives the commentary's patience and inheritance motifs an explicit Christian pairing, though in an eschatological rather than legal estate setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b71d6aec5545624ac28d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.2", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus presumes almsgiving", "note": "Jesus says when giving alms, do not display it for praise; beside the commentary's Qur'anic Jesus commanded to give alms, the Gospel describes how his hearers should give."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a7b94beebe3a8b70318f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.5", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus presumes prayer", "note": "Jesus says when praying, do not perform it for spectators; beside the commentary's Qur'anic Jesus commanded to pray, the Gospel passes prayer instruction to hearers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-caf7f5ce9bf4237aa1b0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.4.9", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "practice what was learned and received", "note": "Paul asks believers to put into practice what they learned, received, heard and saw in him; the inherited word includes conduct for its recipients to enact."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d753161237ce88217d9d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phlm.1.18", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Paul assumes Onesimus's debt", "note": "Paul asks Philemon to charge to him anything Onesimus owes or any wrong he has done; he personally stands behind another's liability like the commentary's kefil."}
{"connection_id": "BC-67e9c344dc6cf523ffcf", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phlm.1.19", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "personal guarantee", "note": "Paul writes in his own hand that he will repay what Onesimus owes; this is a direct Christian instance of the commentary's guarantor personally standing behind another's debt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9222bd1847ac3d0ddc3f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phlm.1.19", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Paul writes his promise to repay", "note": "Paul writes with his own hand that he will repay Onesimus's debt; the guarantor's undertaking becomes an explicit, durable promise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c7ff7ca29e17a483750b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.14.12", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith and endurance of the saints", "note": "The verse identifies the saints by endurance, keeping God's commandments, and faith in Jesus; it brings faith and steadfastness together as a communal witness, though without the commentary's reciprocal advising."}
{"connection_id": "BC-82c68f3f5acb9c4ffa41", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.8.17", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "heirs through suffering", "note": "Paul calls believers heirs of God and co-heirs with Christ, provided they suffer with him; this links inheritance with endurance, a combination central to the commentary's reading of faith and ṣabr."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d69ea371c2cc075cd643", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:29", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mother's exhortation under coercion", "note": "A mother urges her youngest son not to fear execution but to accept death with his brothers; this Jewish martyr story makes counsel in faith under coercion a concrete analogue to the commentary's paired truth and ṣabr."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0bde23e4afd640b8d5b0", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Bava Batra 147b", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "deathbed gift and revocation", "note": "The discussion of a sick person's oral gift asks when it takes effect and whether recovery permits retraction; it supplies a rabbinic legal lens on the commentary's binding testament and its source lexicon's contrasting image of a gift withdrawn."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b484d0bbdad6d61718b5", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Pesahim 56a", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Jacob asks his sons about their faith", "note": "The Talmud reads Jacob's deathbed gathering as his asking whether his sons remain faithful, to which they affirm Israel's God; this is a close Jewish interpretation of the commentary's Jacob asking what his sons will worship after him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7cac645460f46d9a1bfd", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 98:2", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Jacob's interrupted disclosure", "note": "A midrash on Jacob gathering his sons reads the scene as his attempt to reveal the end of days, interrupted when the Divine Presence departs; this shifts the deathbed words from a family bequest toward an eschatological disclosure. Section numbering can vary by edition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4208981be7f58502a952", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 1:1", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "chain of transmission", "note": "The Mishnah opens with Moses receiving Torah and transmitting it through Joshua, elders, prophets, and the assembly; this Jewish tradition makes the commentary's successive handoff of counsel into an explicit chain of teachers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-86f77bc0dcdf2656903c", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 1:1", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Torah handed through named generations", "note": "The rabbinic chain has Moses receive Torah and hand it to Joshua, then elders, prophets and the Great Assembly; it interprets religious instruction as an inherited trust across successive speakers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eb1eec236ada2e36c8d6", "ref": "Mishnah, Shevuot 4:1", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "oath of testimony", "note": "The Mishnah defines the legal scope of an oath of testimony and the witnesses bound by it; it adds a rabbinic procedure for the compulsory witness oath that the commentary develops."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cf2c31477960265ef138", "ref": "Pesachim 56a", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Jacob's sons confess God's unity", "note": "The rabbinic account has the dying Jacob fear a fault in his sons, who answer with the Shema; it turns the patriarch's final exchange into the very question of inherited worship developed from Qur'an 2:133."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d85d2ac2b948deac1747", "ref": "Sirach 29:14", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "stand surety for a neighbor", "note": "The sage praises becoming surety for a neighbor while contrasting it with a shameless person's failure to do so; it supplies a Jewish ethical valuation of the commentary's kefil."}
{"connection_id": "BC-923afeee01ab23e8bb77", "ref": "Sirach 29:15", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remember the surety's costly aid", "note": "The beneficiary is warned not to forget a guarantor who has exposed himself for another; the commentary's surety carries a personal cost beyond merely announcing a pledge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2418e6996875cd86a5c1", "ref": "Sirach 2:4", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "accept hardship patiently, my child", "note": "The address to a child instructs acceptance and patience through humiliation; it joins parental admonition with the precise adversity borne in the commentary's Luqman scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e58ebdac46b2c449afd6", "ref": "Sirach 33:22", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "keep control before distributing inheritance", "note": "The sage says it is better for children to seek from a living parent than for that parent to depend on them; this qualifies the commentary's image of a father trying to retrieve a gift already made."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5e301b33561cd8f60b5f", "ref": "Sirach 33:24", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "instructed timing of inheritance", "note": "The sage advises assigning the inheritance at the end of life; this specifies the commentary's moment when a living person's control becomes a charge to heirs."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7188130835ad587072e2", "ref": "Testament of Job 46–53", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "deathbed distribution", "note": "In the closing testament scene, Job gathers his children and distributes his possessions, with a special inheritance for his daughters; this Jewish pseudepigraphic expansion makes the commentary's link between last counsel and heirs' shares unusually concrete."}

