{"connection_id": "BC-0be3f6542e4913ee3d3d", "ref": "WLC:Exod.22.7", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "entrusted property", "note": "The commentary calls the thing delivered an amanah; Exodus begins a case where money or goods are delivered to a neighbor for safekeeping and then stolen, giving that trust a concrete legal setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a66bee97ca58b16e377c", "ref": "WLC:Exod.22.7", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "custodian answers before God", "note": "If the thief is not found, the householder must come before God about the neighbor's property; custody creates accountable testimony, as in the commentary's deathbed trust."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3a1c76a6eb14ed18188e", "ref": "WLC:Exod.22.8", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "judges resolve disputed entrusted goods", "note": "A dispute over an animal, garment or lost article is brought by both parties before God for judgment; this supplies a procedure when a custodian's claim about entrusted property is contested. This uses WLC numbering."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a93f1c6170a80b006e75", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.1", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "refuse malicious witness partnership", "note": "Israel is forbidden to spread a false report or join a wicked person as a malicious witness; the rule addresses collusion behind the commentary's suspect testimony."}
{"connection_id": "BC-07f7e26056a6b66055df", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.46.16", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prince's gift to son becomes inheritance", "note": "When the prince gives from his property to a son, it belongs to his sons as inherited possession; this tests the commentary's image of a father's gift and heirs' rights."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d9e1427310408563b05", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.46.17", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "gift to servant reverts at release", "note": "A prince's gift to a servant reverts to the prince at the year of liberty, while the son's inheritance in the preceding verse persists; it sharply qualifies the commentary's father who takes back a gift."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b487c3448f6d7ef9bffc", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.6", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "אמנ sound correspondence; Abraham believes", "note": "Abraham trusts the Lord and it is reckoned to him as righteousness; the table's אמנ correspondence links the commentary's faith to a patriarchal act with ethical standing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4273c3db15390ab89346", "ref": "WLC:Gen.18.19", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Abraham commands children after him", "note": "God chooses Abraham so he will command his children and household after him to keep the Lord's way by doing righteousness and justice; faith and right conduct are transmitted together."}
{"connection_id": "BC-39691206837c4c23b30b", "ref": "WLC:Gen.20.18", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ʿ-ṣ-r / Hebrew ʿ-ṣ-r; package table's sound correspondence, unverified", "note": "The Qur'anic word al-ʿaṣr is an oath by time, but the commentary separately reports a source-lexicon gloss under Arabic root ʿ-ṣ-r of giving and then withdrawing a gift; Genesis uses the Hebrew candidate root עָצַר for God closing wombs, a related withholding sense, not the verse's time oath. The sound correspondence is unverified."}
{"connection_id": "BC-352fcb21077b2418cf07", "ref": "WLC:Gen.25.5", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Abraham grants all to Isaac", "note": "Abraham gives all he has to Isaac; the patriarchal transmission of faith in the commentary also has an explicit material inheritance in Genesis."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4103d2efc6a2b8c84cd2", "ref": "WLC:Gen.25.6", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gifts to other sons during life", "note": "Abraham gives gifts to concubines' sons during his life and sends them away from Isaac; the verse shows how a living father distinguishes gifts from the main inheritance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5b62479d35b779ab25bc", "ref": "WLC:Gen.27.33", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "blessing not withdrawn", "note": "The source commentary's dictionary gloss under Arabic root ʿ-ṣ-r (B006) is to give a gift and take it back; after Isaac realizes Jacob received the blessing, he says it will stand, portraying a paternal blessing as irretrievable rather than retracted."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2f35434ed73e804d3b6a", "ref": "WLC:Gen.31.50", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God witnesses treatment of daughters", "note": "Laban tells Jacob that if he mistreats Laban's daughters, God is witness even with no human observer; a father's concern for descendants is secured by testimony beyond his presence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fb38c637e0307a730c3f", "ref": "WLC:Gen.35.2", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jacob instructs household on worship", "note": "Jacob orders his household to put away foreign gods and purify themselves; this supplies an earlier concrete worship instruction beside the commentary's deathbed question about what his sons will worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5cc0d6ed86a5470bcd8d", "ref": "WLC:Gen.41.35", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Joseph stores grain for future need", "note": "Joseph directs grain to be collected under Pharaoh for the coming famine; this supplies the food administration behind the Joseph story's promised load of grain, independently of Hebrew צבר, whose heaping sense is unrelated to Arabic sabr."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c617dbffe6a07c1f5042", "ref": "WLC:Gen.42.37", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Reuben pledges his own sons", "note": "Reuben offers his sons as a guarantee for Benjamin's return; before Judah's surety, the Genesis Joseph story offers a stark alternate form of standing behind another."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0b4b63a72310e4078ee3", "ref": "WLC:Gen.43.9", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "surety for a brother", "note": "Judah tells Jacob that he will be surety for Benjamin and accept blame if he fails to return him; this concretely matches the commentary's image of a guarantor standing behind another's obligation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c87d1c4f91cacaf8019", "ref": "WLC:Gen.43.9", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Judah guarantees Benjamin", "note": "Judah personally stands surety for Benjamin and offers to bear blame if he fails to return him; the Genesis Joseph story supplies a guarantor with a specific obligation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-beffa48dd5a072553ef5", "ref": "WLC:Gen.44.32", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "surety before a father", "note": "Judah says he became surety for the boy to his father and will bear blame if he does not bring him back; the repeated pledge makes the guarantor's personal liability explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d1ef1c0bf87fe5a14cac", "ref": "WLC:Gen.44.32", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Judah recalls his pledge", "note": "Before Joseph, Judah says he became surety for the boy to his father; the guarantor's earlier word remains binding in a later crisis."}
{"connection_id": "BC-167bda87610d37775bac", "ref": "WLC:Gen.44.33", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Judah offers himself in the boy's place", "note": "Judah asks to remain a slave instead of Benjamin, putting his own body behind the guarantee; this makes the commentary's image of a surety standing behind another concrete."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bb8eab97821c546ec7f4", "ref": "WLC:Gen.47.29", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "deathbed charge; oath requested", "note": "The commentary hears 103:3 as a dying person's instruction passed to successors; Jacob, near death, asks Joseph to swear to carry out his burial request, making the bequest an explicit obligation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ea1f20464eccfbecbee7", "ref": "WLC:Gen.47.29", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Jacob's burial charge and oath", "note": "As death approaches Jacob makes Joseph swear to bury him with his fathers; it joins a dying instruction, an heir and a binding oath in one scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-127e9f02ede782e74ee7", "ref": "WLC:Gen.47.30", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "burial instruction", "note": "Jacob specifies that he be carried from Egypt and buried with his fathers; this gives the commentary's otherwise implicit deathbed legacy a concrete patriarchal scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-97c79bb6ccd4d55a9447", "ref": "WLC:Gen.47.31", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "deathbed oath", "note": "Joseph swears to do as Jacob asked, matching the commentary's joining of last instructions with an oath that binds the living after the speaker's death."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b4f56ba34b57e7b98d86", "ref": "WLC:Gen.48.19", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "younger son favored over elder", "note": "Jacob predicts that the younger Ephraim will become greater than Manasseh, reversing birth order in a blessing; this complicates any reading of inherited portions as an unalterable firstborn entitlement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8af6f9bb4e279d1ccbb0", "ref": "WLC:Gen.48.21", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jacob promises God will accompany sons", "note": "As death nears Jacob says God will be with his sons and bring them back to their fathers' land; the parting word carries a future promise beyond the father's life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-abd8c6446ac8999f3813", "ref": "WLC:Gen.48.22", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "extra portion to an heir", "note": "Jacob grants Joseph one portion more than his brothers; this concretely develops the commentary's claim that an inheritance consists of apportioned shares."}
{"connection_id": "BC-370c60e4c9c3298b316f", "ref": "WLC:Gen.48.5", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grandsons adopted as heirs", "note": "Jacob declares Joseph's two sons to be his own sons for inheritance; this extends the commentary's discussion of who receives a share beyond the obvious line of sons."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c0e5779204956b838943", "ref": "WLC:Gen.49.1", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Jacob summons sons before death", "note": "Jacob calls his sons to hear what will happen to them; the commentary reads mutual counsel as a word handed on at the edge of death, while its cited Qur'anic Jacob asks specifically whom they will worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8a5ab2a0d2e2aecb5154", "ref": "WLC:Gen.49.28", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "father's blessing distributed among heirs", "note": "Jacob blesses each of his twelve sons according to his blessing; this is a distinct patriarchal testament in which the father's last words are distributed among successors."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f0741a4f5fbbba7b1f77", "ref": "WLC:Gen.50.24", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "final instruction; future generation", "note": "Dying Joseph tells his brothers that God will bring them from Egypt to the land promised their ancestors; like the commentary's counsel passed onward, his last words orient successors toward an inherited promise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ea60d4d788994eac25e7", "ref": "WLC:Gen.50.25", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bones entrusted by oath", "note": "Joseph makes Israel's sons swear to carry his bones when God visits them; the oath preserves a charge and a physical trust across generations, beyond the speaker's life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ca385c6dc3c7ba6d5e0", "ref": "WLC:Gen.50.25", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Joseph binds descendants by oath", "note": "Dying Joseph makes Israel's sons swear to carry his bones away when God visits them; an obligation is entrusted to a later generation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c45d6e262b8dd1b4ca6d", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.4", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "אמנ sound correspondence; fidelity through crisis", "note": "The righteous lives by faithfulness while facing the proud oppressor; Hebrew אֱמוּנָה from the table connects the commentary's belief with endurance under strain."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-23b8fc234efae27d0158", "ref": "WLC:Isa.10.1", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Arabic ḥ-q-q / Hebrew ḥ-q-q; package table's sound correspondence, unverified", "note": "The commentary labels بِٱلْحَقّ as a right binding on heirs; Isaiah condemns חֹקְקֵי אָוֶן, those writing unjust statutes that deny the poor justice, showing that prescribed law can oppose the right the commentary describes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cce8337fcfb62a3e8a85", "ref": "WLC:Isa.10.1", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "חקק sound correspondence; unjust decrees", "note": "Isaiah condemns those who decree oppressive decrees; Hebrew חקק from the table shows that a binding rule can itself be unjust, sharpening the commentary's concern about an unfair will."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5ef7a7029066d5b5419b", "ref": "WLC:Isa.10.2", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unjust decrees dispossess widows and orphans", "note": "The continuation says the decrees deprive the needy of justice and make widows and orphans prey; it specifies whose rights are injured when prescribed allocations are corrupted."}
{"connection_id": "BC-083946b6b0c348760522", "ref": "WLC:Isa.28.16", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "אמנ sound correspondence; believer does not hurry", "note": "The one who trusts the tested cornerstone will not hurry in panic; Hebrew אמנ from the table joins believing with a concrete form of patient steadiness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eec999e655c5f2f0cba6", "ref": "WLC:Isa.7.9", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "אמנ sound correspondence; trust and firmness", "note": "Isaiah warns that if the addressed king and house do not trust, they will not stand firm; the verse plays on אמנ twice, making steadfastness the practical stake of belief."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4177b6248fb9e5e47509", "ref": "WLC:Jer.32.10", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "witnessed property deed", "note": "Jeremiah signs, seals, and has witnesses attest a purchase deed; the legal transfer gives the commentary's rights of heirs and witness scene a concrete documentary analogue."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8bcab21aa5ad368d163a", "ref": "WLC:Jer.32.14", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "deed preserved for the future", "note": "The deed is placed in an earthen vessel so it may last many days; this closely matches the commentary's entrusted legacy preserved for successors."}
{"connection_id": "BC-29f3e66e574ad44ed413", "ref": "WLC:Jer.32.15", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "land to be possessed again", "note": "Jeremiah's witnessed deed is interpreted by the promise that houses, fields, and vineyards will again be bought in the land; this frames the preserved document as a promise to future inheritors."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ddab46286a1e55a6d60d", "ref": "WLC:Jer.35.14", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jonadab's words observed to this day", "note": "God cites the Rechabites' continuing obedience to their ancestor's instruction as a rebuke to Judah's failure to heed repeated divine speech; inherited counsel becomes a measure of faithfulness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-69b7ecde8870cf6562f2", "ref": "WLC:Jer.35.6", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ancestor's injunction still binds descendants", "note": "The Rechabites refuse wine because their ancestor Jonadab commanded them and their children never to drink it; a concrete instruction continues after its speaker's death."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4f910fb896059a118001", "ref": "WLC:Jer.36.4", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prophet entrusts his words to a scribe", "note": "Baruch writes the words Jeremiah dictates on a scroll; the commentary's advice passed to another to act becomes a specific transfer of a prophet's speech."}
{"connection_id": "BC-effcf6055a3c3a440ff1", "ref": "WLC:Jer.36.5", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "restrained speaker must use a delegate", "note": "Jeremiah says he is barred from entering the Lord's house, so Baruch must carry and read the message; a speaker's inability to act makes a successor's faithful performance necessary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9951eb3d96df6f6da3fe", "ref": "WLC:Job.17.3", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "plea for someone to be surety", "note": "Job asks God for a pledge and who will give surety for him; the legal image resembles the commentary's kefil, though Job's disputed standing before God differs from Joseph's announced reward."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5a04b817b51c1f1c4d7a", "ref": "WLC:Job.19.23", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "חקק sound correspondence; enduring inscribed words", "note": "Job wishes his words written and inscribed in a book; the table's חקק verb gives a concrete form to the commentary's word left behind, though Job seeks a record of his protest rather than an heir's instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-37e43337ac465744aec1", "ref": "WLC:Job.27.16", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wicked person piles up silver", "note": "A wicked person heaps silver and stores clothing, but the following verse assigns them to others; the commentary's estate can outlast the owner without preserving the owner's intended control."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c867210f78dbb4d64e44", "ref": "WLC:Job.27.17", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "others divide accumulated goods", "note": "The righteous will wear the stored clothing and the innocent divide the silver; this reverses the accumulator's claim to determine who receives his goods."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a792cddfc91e2d4eb389", "ref": "WLC:Josh.1.7", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Joshua inherits law with courage", "note": "Joshua is told to be strong and carefully do all the law Moses commanded him; a successor receives both an actionable charge and the endurance needed to carry it out."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c84db0f2ee1d548728f8", "ref": "WLC:Josh.23.14", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Joshua speaks as his death nears", "note": "Joshua says he is going the way of all the earth and reminds Israel that God's promises have not failed; a leader's last speech preserves a standard for successors."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1cf7404f0352ade20ba3", "ref": "WLC:Josh.24.15", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "choose whom your household will serve", "note": "Joshua sets before Israel a choice of whom to serve and declares his household's allegiance; this is a public answer to the commentary's question of worship after a leader departs."}
{"connection_id": "BC-592f79af904bb629e484", "ref": "WLC:Lev.5.1", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "witness under public adjuration", "note": "A person who hears an adjuration and can testify but fails to tell what was seen or known bears guilt; oath and the duty to disclose meet in the commentary's witness scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a61f1862f32dd226e5b4", "ref": "WLC:Lev.5.21", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "denial of an entrusted deposit", "note": "The law treats deception over a neighbor's deposited goods as a breach against the Lord; it gives moral and legal weight to the commentary's emanet left in another's keeping. This uses WLC numbering."}
{"connection_id": "BC-01711f18226595316051", "ref": "WLC:Lev.5.22", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "false oath about another's property", "note": "The same law names finding lost property, denying it and swearing falsely; the commentary's suspicion of corrupt sworn testimony concerns an equally concrete property dispute. This uses WLC numbering."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2658e563ca109273d1cc", "ref": "WLC:Lev.5.24", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "restitution after property deception", "note": "The wrongdoer must restore the property in full and add a fifth when guilt is acknowledged; the commentary's correction of an unjust transfer has a concrete restitution analogue. This uses WLC numbering."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3351a15fa37ca465f61d", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.16", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mutual speech and memorial", "note": "Those who fear God speak with one another, God listens, and a book of remembrance is written; the scene closely joins the commentary's mutual counsel with a record that preserves the speakers beyond the moment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-acf89e8af71d917f360d", "ref": "WLC:Neh.9.8", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "אמנ sound correspondence; Abraham's faithful heart", "note": "The prayer says God found Abraham's heart faithful and made covenant with him; the table's אמנ family joins the patriarch's trust to a promise carried to descendants."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b36af879c507df5af523", "ref": "WLC:Num.27.11", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic ḥ-q-q / Hebrew ḥ-q-q; package table's sound correspondence, unverified", "note": "The commentary labels بِٱلْحَقّ with Arabic root ḥ-q-q and calls heirs' shares rights; Numbers gives a succession rule for the nearest relative and calls it חֻקַּת מִשְׁפָּט, a prescribed judgment, showing how a cognate candidate's legal sense can secure inheritance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-870a4c38edd2bd4244e0", "ref": "WLC:Num.27.11", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "חקק sound correspondence; inheritance statute", "note": "The inheritance sequence ends by making its order a fixed statute of judgment; Hebrew חֻקַּת from the table's חקק correspondence links prescribed law to the commentary's binding haqq, without equating the two senses."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3e618eac1064edef1f83", "ref": "WLC:Num.27.7", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "daughters' rightful inheritance", "note": "God declares Zelophehad's daughters' claim right and commands their father's inheritance be transferred to them; the commentary treats heirs' portions as enforceable rights."}
{"connection_id": "BC-729093c000730e801772", "ref": "WLC:Num.30.3", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "spoken vow becomes obligation", "note": "A person who vows to the Lord or binds himself by oath must not break his word but do what he said; the commentary's binding testament and weighty oath meet in the force of an utterance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dfa401807c1ab81b86c5", "ref": "WLC:Prov.1.8", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "parental teaching addressed to a son", "note": "A son is instructed to hear his father's discipline and not abandon his mother's teaching; the form of address matches the commentary's Luqman instructing his son."}
{"connection_id": "BC-77029ad1ed10f4a5549f", "ref": "WLC:Prov.11.15", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "surety's risk", "note": "Proverbs warns that one who guarantees a stranger will suffer; beside the commentary's approving image of the guarantor standing behind another, it exposes the personal risk carried by that role."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d96e15ac415667fd0f6d", "ref": "WLC:Prov.11.15", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cost of standing surety", "note": "The proverb warns that one who guarantees a stranger suffers, while refusal is secure; it supplies the risk implicit in the commentary's surety image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-01a2fafa314746e6c873", "ref": "WLC:Prov.13.22", "target": "sec10", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "inheritance to grandchildren", "note": "The good person leaves an inheritance to children's children; this states directly the intergenerational transfer that the commentary uses to illuminate mutual counsel as a legacy."}
